china's fintech giant ant group was on  pace to break ipo records  and on track to raise more than 34  billion dollars  ahead of the blockbuster listings in  hong kong and shanghai  the company's charismatic co-founder  jack ma spoke at a public forum about  how  china's financial system should be best  regulated  in a dramatic turnaround chinese  regulators brought the ipo plans to a  halt  casting doubt over the future of am so  how did plans for what would have been  the world's biggest ipo  come screeching to a hall  and has revolutionized digital finance  in china  there is nothing quite like it in  western financial circles  for china's leadership it is a source of  great pride that you have these big  powerful tech companies like anne it  handles more than 17 trillion dollars a  year in payments  it really is one of the backbones of the  e-commerce industry  many people save and invest through anne  and they also use it to borrow money as  well  it has kind of stepped in and assumed  this important position  between banks and their end customers  where it has this nice  business of kind of organizing loans  whereas the banks bear most of the  credit risk  ant has come under increasing regulatory  scrutiny partly because of its  sheer size some of the biggest potential  problems for ant  center on its loan business draft rules  covering lending done by businesses  other than banks  effectively require the company to put  more of its own skin in the game  now it needs perhaps to put as much as  30 of the  capital up itself recently ant's  co-founder jack ma  has made rather provocative speech where  he talked about how he thought the  financial system in  china should be overseen  and that perhaps was rather unwelcome  in the highest circles of the chinese  leadership  but we don't know precisely why  regulators decided to suspend the ipo  but we know that there was a meeting  between  top and executives jack ma and four of  china's major financial regulators  and a day later the stock exchange said  that actually this  couldn't go ahead for now it seems to  some observers that this is partly also  a way of  letting ant's top leaders and  controlling shareholder jack ma know  that they shouldn't  get too big for their britches in a way  that they shouldn't presume to know  what's best for  china's financial system or what kind of  regulation works best in the country  where the government likes to keep a  very tight  control on things in almost every  industry  the reaction in the markets basically  was one of shocks  shares in alibaba which owns part of ant  fell shares in the hong kong stock  exchange fell because  it was due to see a boost in trading  volumes after ant  went public and apologized to investors  for any trouble caused  and said it would embrace regulations it  has outlined how it will refund  individual investors in hong kong  investors in mainland china are still  waiting for details on how they will get  their money back  and has to kind of go back and think  about what it needs to redo  in order to kind of win the approval of  these all-powerful chinese regulators to  go public
 

